::ALG2_U01_L07_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L07_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>A rectangle has width x and length x + 6. Its area is 91. Which equation models the situation? {
~x + x + 6 = 91 #This adds dimensions instead of multiplying for area.
=x(x + 6) = 91 #Correct. Area is width times length.
~x(x - 6) = 91 #The length is x + 6, not x - 6.
~x + 6 = 91 #This only uses the length expression.
}

::ALG2_U01_L07_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L07_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x(x + 6) = 91 and identify the reasonable width. {
~x = -13 #This is an algebraic solution, but a width cannot be negative.
=x = 7 #Correct. x squared plus 6x minus 91 factors as (x + 13)(x - 7), so the reasonable width is 7.
~x = 13 #This is related to the rejected solution, not the width.
~x = 91 #This is the area, not the width.
}

::ALG2_U01_L07_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L07_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>A garden has width x and length x + 4. Its area is 45. What is the reasonable width? {
~x = -9 #This solves the equation but is not a reasonable width.
=x = 5 #Correct. x(x + 4) = 45 gives x squared plus 4x minus 45 = 0, so x = 5 or -9.
~x = 9 #This is the length when x = 5, not the width.
~x = 45 #This is the area.
}

::ALG2_U01_L07_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L07_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>A product design has area model x<sup>2</sup> - 2x - 35 = 0. Which solution is reasonable for a positive measurement? {
~x = -5 #A negative measurement is not reasonable in this context.
=x = 7 #Correct. The equation factors as (x - 7)(x + 5) = 0, and x = 7 is positive.
~x = 35 #This is the constant term, not a solution.
~x = -7 #This is not a solution of the equation.
}

::ALG2_U01_L07_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L07_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>The height model h(t) = -16t<sup>2</sup> + 80t equals 0 at which times? {
~t = 0 and t = 4 #This would match -16t squared plus 64t.
=t = 0 and t = 5 #Correct. Factor -16t(t - 5) = 0.
~t = 5 only #This leaves out the starting time t = 0.
~t = 80 only #This is a coefficient, not a solution.
}

::ALG2_U01_L07_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L07_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>For h(t) = -16t<sup>2</sup> + 80t, what does t = 0 represent? {
=The starting time when the object is on the ground. #Correct. h(0) = 0, so the object starts at ground height.
~The time the object returns to the ground. #The later zero, t = 5, represents the return time.
~The maximum height. #A time of 0 is not the maximum height.
~The object's speed. #The variable t represents time, not speed.
}

::ALG2_U01_L07_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L07_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>For h(t) = -16t<sup>2</sup> + 80t, what does t = 5 represent? {
~The starting time. #The starting time is t = 0.
=The time when the object returns to the ground. #Correct. h(t) = 0 at t = 5 after launch.
~The maximum height in feet. #t = 5 is a time, not a height.
~The horizontal distance. #The model gives height as a function of time.
}

::ALG2_U01_L07_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L07_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>A volume model is V(x) = x<sup>3</sup> - 9x. Which positive solution makes V(x) = 0? {
~x = -3 #This is a solution but not positive.
~x = 0 #This is not positive.
=x = 3 #Correct. Factor x(x - 3)(x + 3), and the positive solution is 3.
~x = 9 #This is from the coefficient, not from solving.
}

::ALG2_U01_L07_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L07_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>A volume model is V(x) = x<sup>3</sup> + 2x<sup>2</sup> - 8x. Which positive solution makes V(x) = 0? {
~x = -4 #This is a solution but not positive.
~x = 0 #This is not a positive dimension.
=x = 2 #Correct. Factor x(x + 4)(x - 2), and the positive solution is 2.
~x = 4 #This is not a solution.
}

::ALG2_U01_L07_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L07_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which tool is most useful for solving x<sup>3</sup> - 9x = 0 in a modeling context? {
~Completing the square #This is usually used for quadratics, not this factored cubic structure.
=Factoring out the greatest common factor #Correct. Factoring out x gives x(x squared - 9) = 0.
~Dividing by x immediately #This can lose the solution x = 0.
~Ignoring the equation #The equation must be solved and interpreted.
}

::ALG2_U01_L07_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L07_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>A model gives solutions x = -8 and x = 3 for a length. Which solution should be used? {
~x = -8 #A length cannot be negative in this context.
=x = 3 #Correct. Positive length is reasonable.
~Both solutions #Only the positive measurement is reasonable.
~Neither solution #x = 3 is reasonable.
}

::ALG2_U01_L07_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L07_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>A model gives solutions t = 0 and t = 6 for time. Which interpretation is usually correct in a projectile context? {
=t = 0 is the start time and t = 6 is the return time. #Correct. Both can be meaningful times when height is 0.
~t = 0 must always be rejected. #Starting time can be meaningful.
~t = 6 must always be rejected. #The later time can be meaningful.
~Both times are complex. #Both times are real.
}

::ALG2_U01_L07_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L07_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>A quadratic model for a real measurement has no real solutions. What is the best interpretation? {
~The measurement must be negative. #No real solutions does not mean the measurement is negative.
=There is no real measurement that satisfies the model. #Correct. A real-world measurement requires a real value.
~Every real number works. #No real solutions means no real value works.
~The model is automatically linear. #A quadratic model remains quadratic.
}

::ALG2_U01_L07_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L07_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement best describes why context is checked after solving? {
~All algebraic solutions are always reasonable. #Some algebraic solutions may not make sense in context.
=A solution can be algebraically correct but unreasonable in the situation. #Correct. Context can rule out values such as negative lengths.
~Context changes the rules of algebra. #The algebra is still valid; interpretation is the issue.
~Only positive solutions are ever allowed. #Some contexts allow zero or negative values.
}

::ALG2_U01_L07_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L07_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which model represents "a number multiplied by 4 more than the number is 60"? {
~x + 4x = 60 #This adds the number and four times the number.
=x(x + 4) = 60 #Correct. The number is x, and 4 more than the number is x + 4.
~4(x + 60) = x #This does not match the phrase.
~x - 4 = 60 #This only represents 4 less than a number.
}

::ALG2_U01_L07_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L07_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Solve x(x + 4) = 60 and choose the reasonable positive value. {
~x = -10 #This is an algebraic solution but not the positive value.
=x = 6 #Correct. x squared plus 4x minus 60 factors as (x + 10)(x - 6).
~x = 10 #This is not a solution of the equation.
~x = 60 #This is the product, not the value of x.
}

::ALG2_U01_L07_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L07_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression represents the area of a rectangle with width x - 2 and length x + 3? {
~x - 2 + x + 3 #This adds dimensions rather than multiplying for area.
=(x - 2)(x + 3) #Correct. Area is length times width.
~x(x + 3) - 2 #This does not represent multiplying both dimensions.
~x<sup>2</sup> + 6 #This is not the product of the binomials.
}

::ALG2_U01_L07_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L07_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Expand (x - 2)(x + 3) for use in a model. {
~x<sup>2</sup> + x + 6 #The constant term should be -6.
=x<sup>2</sup> + x - 6 #Correct. Multiply to get x squared plus 3x minus 2x minus 6.
~x<sup>2</sup> - x - 6 #The middle term should be positive x.
~x<sup>2</sup> + 5x - 6 #The middle terms are 3x and -2x, which combine to x.
}

::ALG2_U01_L07_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L07_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>A model has equation x<sup>2</sup> + x - 6 = 0. Which solutions are correct? {
~x = -1 and x = 6 #These do not come from the factors.
=x = -3 and x = 2 #Correct. The equation factors as (x + 3)(x - 2) = 0.
~x = 3 and x = -2 #These have the signs reversed.
~x = 0 and x = 6 #Zero is not a solution.
}

::ALG2_U01_L07_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L07_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>If x represents the width of a rectangle, which solution from x<sup>2</sup> + x - 6 = 0 is reasonable? {
~x = -3 #A rectangle width cannot be negative.
=x = 2 #Correct. The positive solution is the reasonable width.
~Both x = -3 and x = 2 #Only the positive width is reasonable.
~Neither solution #x = 2 is reasonable.
}

::ALG2_U01_L07_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L07_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>A context asks for the time when an object first launches and later lands. Which solutions from h(t) = -16t(t - 3) are meaningful? {
~t = 3 only #The launch time can also be meaningful.
=t = 0 and t = 3 #Correct. t = 0 is launch time and t = 3 is landing time.
~t = -3 and t = 0 #The factor t - 3 gives t = 3, not -3.
~No solutions #The factored model gives two real times.
}

::ALG2_U01_L07_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L07_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which value should be rejected if x represents a side length and the equation gives x = 4 and x = -9? {
~x = 4 #A positive side length can be reasonable.
=x = -9 #Correct. A side length cannot be negative.
~Both values #The positive value can be reasonable.
~Neither value #The negative value should be rejected.
}

::ALG2_U01_L07_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L07_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which information is most important when interpreting a solution? {
~Only the largest coefficient #Coefficients matter, but interpretation depends on context.
=What the variable represents #Correct. The meaning and units of the variable guide interpretation.
~The alphabetical order of variables #Alphabetical order does not determine meaning.
~The number of answer choices #Answer choice count does not determine interpretation.
}

::ALG2_U01_L07_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L07_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>A model gives complex solutions for a physical length. What is the best conclusion? {
~Use the imaginary part as the length. #A physical length in this context requires a real value.
=The model has no real length solution for the given conditions. #Correct. Complex non-real solutions are not physical lengths.
~Every length works. #Complex solutions do not mean every real length works.
~The length must be zero. #Complex solutions do not automatically mean zero.
}

::ALG2_U01_L07_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L07_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Support-only review; no new primary MLA standard<br><br>Question:<br>Which response best completes a modeling solution? {
~Only list the algebraic answer with no units. #A modeling answer should include interpretation and units when appropriate.
=State the solution, units, and whether it is reasonable in context. #Correct. Modeling requires both solving and interpreting.
~Reject every negative number in every context. #Some contexts can allow negative values.
~Ignore restrictions after solving. #Restrictions are part of the interpretation.
}
